    'title': "The Lion's Bath: A Tale from the Bamoun Kingdom",
    'slug': 'lions-bath',
    'content':
        '''# The Lion's Bath\n\nIn the great kingdom of Bamoun, there lived a mighty lion named King Tabondo. He was the strongest of all the animals, and his roar could shake the mountains.\n\nBut King Tabondo had a secret: he was afraid of water.\n\nEvery dry season, when the rivers ran low and the watering holes shrank, the animals grew desperate. They begged King Tabondo to share his secret spring — the only deep water left in the kingdom.\n\n"I will share it," King Tabondo declared, "but only if someone can make me laugh. I have not laughed in ten years!"\n\nThe animals came one by one. The elephant told jokes about his trunk. The hyena told his funniest stories. The monkey performed acrobatics. But King Tabondo did not laugh.\n\nThen came Tama the rabbit, small and quick, with bright eyes and a mischievous smile.\n\n"I have a riddle for you, King Tabondo," said Tama. "What is so strong that even the lion fears it, yet so gentle that even a child can hold it?"\n\nKing Tabondo thought hard. "I do not know. Tell me."\n\n"Water," said Tama with a grin. "For even the mighty lion avoids the bath!"\n\nFor the first time in ten years, King Tabondo laughed. A deep, thundering laugh that echoed across the savannah.\n\n"You have earned the right," he said. "Drink from my spring, all of you. And Tama — you shall be my advisor, for you have shown that wisdom is mightier than strength."\n\nFrom that day on, King Tabondo learned to enjoy the water. And the animals of the kingdom never went thirsty again.\n\n**Moral:** Wisdom and cleverness can overcome even the greatest fears and strongest opponents.''',
