  {
    'title': 'The Talking Drum of Foumban',
    'slug': 'talking-drum-foumban',
    'content':
        '''# The Talking Drum of Foumban\n\nIn the heart of Foumban, the ancient capital of the Bamoun people, there stood a drum unlike any other. It was carved from the wood of the iroko tree by the greatest drummer who ever lived — Nji Kuma, the grandfather of rhythm.\n\nThis drum had a voice. Not the voice of wood and skin, but a true voice — the voice of truth.\n\nWhen Nji Kuma played the drum, it would speak. It would tell stories of the ancestors, warn of danger, and sing songs of joy. The people of Foumban would gather in the great square to hear the drum speak.\n\nBut as Nji Kuma grew old, he worried. "Who will play the drum when I am gone?" he asked. "Who will carry the voice of truth?"\n\nHis apprentice, a young man named Kwame, volunteered. But when Kwame played the drum, it said nothing. Just the sound of wood and skin.\n\n"You have skill," Nji Kuma told him, "but you do not have the heart. The drum speaks only to those who carry truth in their souls."\n\nKwame spent many years learning to live with honesty and integrity. He helped the poor, spoke truthfully even when it was difficult, and never took what was not his.\n\nOne night, alone in the temple, Kwame played the drum. And for the first time, it spoke.\n\n"You have earned the right," the drum said. "I will speak through you, and you will carry the voice of truth to all the people."\n\nFrom that day on, Kwame became the voice of Foumban. He played the drum at festivals, at councils, and at ceremonies. And the people listened, because they knew the drum never lied.\n\nToday, in the Royal Palace Museum of Foumban, you can see the drum. It is silent now, but the people say that on quiet nights, you can still hear it whispering the stories of the ancestors.\n\n**Moral:** Truth and integrity are the foundations of trust and leadership.''',
