    'title': 'The River Goddess of the Sanaga',
    'slug': 'river-goddess',
    'content':
        '''# The River Goddess of the Sanaga\n\nThe Sanaga is the longest river in Cameroon, flowing from the Adamawa Plateau to the Atlantic Ocean. The people who live along its banks tell stories of Mami Wata — the Water Goddess.\n\nOne evening, a fisherman named Kotto was paddling his canoe near the rapids when he saw a beautiful woman sitting on a rock in the middle of the river. Her skin shimmered like fish scales, and her hair flowed like water itself.\n\n"Kotto," she said, "I have watched you fish these waters for many years. You always take only what you need, and you never fish during spawning season. Because of your respect for the river, I will grant you a wish."\n\nKotto thought carefully. He could wish for wealth, or power, or long life. But instead, he said: "I wish for the river to always be healthy, so that my children and their children can always find fish."\n\nThe goddess smiled. "You are wise, Kotto. Many would wish for selfish things. Because you asked for the health of the river rather than yourself, your wish is granted."\n\nFrom that day on, the Sanaga was the healthiest river in all the land. Fish were abundant, the water was clean, and the people who lived along its banks prospered.\n\nBut Kotto added a warning: "If anyone takes more than they need, or pollutes the water, the goddess will withdraw her blessing."\n\nAnd so the people learned to live in harmony with the river, taking only what they needed and protecting the waters for future generations.\n\n**Moral:** Respect for nature brings abundance. Greed and carelessness bring ruin.''',
