    'content':
        '''# The Weaver Bird and the Lion\n\nHigh in the branches of the great baobab tree, a weaver bird named Sio built the most beautiful nest anyone had ever seen. It was intricate, strong, and hung like a jewel from the highest branch.\n\nOne day, a mighty lion named Kossa walked beneath the tree. He looked up and saw the nest.\n\n"Beautiful work, little bird," Kossa said. "But I could crush it with one paw."\n\nSio was not afraid. "You could, Great Lion. But then who would sing the morning songs that wake the savannah?"\n\nThe lion paused. He thought about the songs — the beautiful melodies that filled the air at dawn, the songs that made the flowers open and the children smile.\n\n"You are right, little one," Kossa said softly. "Every creature has its gift. Yours is beauty. Mine is strength. But neither is complete without the other."\n\nFrom that day, the lion protected the weaver bird's nest, and the weaver bird sang songs of gratitude every morning.\n\nAnd the people of the village learned from them: that strength without gentleness is cruel, and gentleness without strength is vulnerable.\n\n**Moral:** Every creature has a unique gift. True strength lies in protecting the gifts of others.''',
